MQ_UNLINK(2)                BSD System Calls Manual               MQ_UNLINK(2)

NNAAMMEE
     mmqq__uunnlliinnkk — mq_unlink - remove a message queue (REALTIME)

LLIIBBRRAARRYY
     POSIX Real-time Library (librt, -lrt)

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ##iinncclluuddee <<mmqquueeuuee..hh>>

     _i_n_t
     mmqq__uunnlliinnkk(_c_o_n_s_t _c_h_a_r _*_n_a_m_e);

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The mmqq__uunnlliinnkk() function removes the message queue named by the string
     _n_a_m_e.  If one or more processes have the message queue open when
     mmqq__uunnlliinnkk() is called, destruction of the message queue will be postponed
     until all references to the message queue have been closed.  However, the
     mmqq__uunnlliinnkk() call need not block until all references have been closed; it
     may return immediately.

     After a successful call to mmqq__uunnlliinnkk(), reuse of the name will subse‐
     quently cause mq_open(2) to behave as if no message queue of this name
     exists.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     Upon successful completion, the value 0 is returned; otherwise the
     value -1 is returned and the global variable _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the
     error.

EERRRROORRSS
     The mmqq__uunnlliinnkk() system call will fail if:

     [EACCESS]          Permission is denied to unlink the message queue rep‐
                        resented by _n_a_m_e.

     [EINVAL]           _n_a_m_e is invalid.

     [ENAMETOOLONG]     The length of the _n_a_m_e argument exceeds {PATH_MAX} or
                        a pathname component is longer than {NAME_MAX}.

     [ENOENT]           The message queue does not exist.

     [ENOSYS]           mqueuefs(5) module is neither loaded nor included in
                        the kernel.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     mq_open(2)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The mmqq__uunnlliinnkk() system call conforms to IEEE Std 1003.1-2004 (“POSIX.1”).
     The [EACCESS] error code is an extension to the standard.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     Support for POSIX message queues first appeared in FreeBSD 7.0.

